-- title:
--   Vanishing of dlogΨ on ordinary corner finite-part classes
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $p \neq 2$ is a prime and $M$ a positive integer with $p \mid M$ and $p^2 \nmid M$ (`hpM`, `hpM2`), $H \le (\mathbf Z/M)^\times$ is a subgroup containing every unit whose image in $(\mathbf Z/(M/p))^\times$ is $1$ (`hHp`), and $S \subseteq \mathbf N$ is a set of primes to be excluded from the Hecke generators. The hypothesis `hin` is the predicate `HeckeDiamondInputsHAll M H`: for every prime $\ell$ the operator-input condition `HeckeInputsHAlong` holds for $X_H(M)$ over $\overline{\mathbf Q}$ at $\ell$, and every $d \in (\mathbf Z/M)^\times$ is realised by an $\overline{\mathbf Q}$-algebra automorphism $\sigma$ of $\overline F_H =$ `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` satisfying `IsDiamondAutHBar M H d`. Here $J_H(M) =$ `JH M H` is $\operatorname{Pic}^0$ of $\overline F_H$, i.e. degree-zero divisors on the places of $\overline F_H$ modulo principal ones, and `genOpH M H S g` is the endomorphism of $J_H(M)$ attached to a generator symbol $g$ of [`CohCarrier.Gen M S`](def/CohCarrier_Inst.html#L13) (symbols $T_\ell$ for $\ell$ prime, $\ell \notin S$, $\ell \nmid M$; $U_q$ for $q$ prime dividing $M$; $\langle d\rangle$ for $d \in (\mathbf Z/M)^\times$).
--
--   **Hecke algebra and ordinary corner.** $\mathbf T$ is a commutative ring which is a $\mathbf Z_p$-algebra and acts on the Tate module [`TateModule p (JH M H)`](def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L15) (sequences $(x_n)_{n\in\mathbf N}$ in $J_H(M)$ with $p^n x_n = 0$ and $p\,x_{n+1} = x_n$) compatibly with the $\mathbf Z_p$-action; `hfaith` says the action is faithful; `op` assigns to each generator symbol an element of $\mathbf T$ acting as the induced endomorphism `tateGenOpH M H S p g` of the Tate module (`hop`), and `hgen` says the $\mathbf Z_p$-subalgebra generated by the range of `op` is all of $\mathbf T$. $S'$ is an [`IharaLemma.IdempotentSplitting`](def/IharaLemma_IdempotentSplitting.html#L7) of $\mathbf T$: a finite family of idempotents $e_i$ which are completely orthogonal, together with maximal ideals $\mathfrak m_i$ exhausting all maximal ideals of $\mathbf T$ and satisfying $e_i \in \mathfrak m_j \iff i \neq j$. The index $i_0$ is ordinary in the sense that `hord` requires $\operatorname{op}(U_p) \notin \mathfrak m_{i_0}$.
--
--   **Local data at $p$ and integral models.** $Pl$ is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbf Q}$ with $p$ a non-unit of $Pl$ (`hPl`), whose residue field is algebraically closed of characteristic $p$; $K$ is an algebraically closed field which is an algebra over $\mathbf Z/p$ and over the residue field of $Pl$. The hypothesis `hj` places the $q$-expansion of $j$ in the level-$\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbf Z)$ $q$-expansion function field over $\mathbf Q$, which is what makes the two-chart integral models available. $\mathfrak X$ is an `XHDRModelAtP` for $(p,M,H)$: a proper flat integral model of $X_H(M)$ over $R_p$ together with a smooth model at level $\Gamma_N$, a curve model `𝔛.Meta` of $\overline F_H$ over $\overline{\mathbf Q}$ with its bijection `pointEquivPlace` between $\overline{\mathbf Q}$-points and places, the comparison isomorphism `𝔛.eeta` with the generic geometric fibre, and the further structure (cusp section `εinf`, degeneracy maps `π`, `πw`, Atkin–Lehner isomorphism `w`, diamonds `dia0`, special-fibre curve model `Mfib` with its component maps, smooth locus) recorded in that structure. $\Lambda$ is a `JHNeronObjectAtP.LevelData` at the lowered level: a map $\sigma_\Lambda$ from $\operatorname{Spec} Pl$ to the base lifting the generic point, a scheme $\Lambda.X$ over the base with a relative group law, a bijection $\Lambda.\mathrm{pts}$ between $J_{H'}(M/p)$ (with $H' =$ `infSubgroup p M H hpM`, the image of $H$ in $(\mathbf Z/(M/p))^\times$) and sections over the generic point, and a bijection $\Lambda.\mathrm{ptsSp}$ between $\operatorname{Pic}^0$ of the level-$\Gamma_N$ $q$-expansion function field $\overline F'$ over the residue field of $Pl$ and sections over the residue point. The hypothesis `hrepΛ` asserts that the designation built from $(\Lambda.X,\Lambda.f)$ with the unit section represents the relative $\operatorname{Pic}^0$ subfunctor cut out by the fibrewise algebraically-trivial condition `algEquivZeroCut` for the $\Gamma_N$-level curve with section `𝔛.εinf ≫ 𝔛.π`. $O$ is a `JHNeronObjectAtP` over this data: a smooth, separated, surjective group scheme $O.g : O.G \to$ base with relative commutative group law, a bijection $O.\mathrm{pts}$ of $J_H(M)$ with sections over the generic point which is additive and Galois- and Hecke-equivariant, together with its flatness and properness clauses, its toric rank, its supersingular pairing set `O.ssFinset`, and the subgroups `O.toricPts m` and `O.finPts m`, the latter being the subgroup generated by those $m$-torsion classes $x$ of $\operatorname{Pic}^0(\overline F_H)$ for which the section $O.\mathrm{pts}\,x$ satisfies the extension predicate `ExtendsToPlace` over $Pl$.
--
--   **Abel–Jacobi configuration** (`hD`, `hDQ`, `hsep`, `ajQ`, `kQ`, `ajbar`, `εbar`, `hpoinc`, `hajQε`, `hajQ`, `hkQ₁`, `hkQ₂`, `hajbar`, `hajbar_over`, `hεbar`, `hεbar_aj`, `hpts_law`, `hAJ`). The designation built from $(O.G, O.g)$ with the unit section represents the `algEquivZeroCut` subfunctor for the $\Gamma_M$-level curve with section `𝔛.εinf`, and its base change to $\mathbf Q$ represents the corresponding subfunctor for the base-changed curve with the base-changed section; the base-changed curve is separated. Over $\mathbf Q$ there is a morphism `ajQ` from the curve to the base of the base-changed designation, compatible with the structure maps, carrying the cusp section to the zero section (`hajQε`) and having the Abel–Jacobi property `hajQ`: for every field $k$, every $k$-point $t$ of $\operatorname{Spec}\mathbf Q$ and every $k$-point $x$ of the curve over $t$, the pullback of the Poincaré bundle along $x$ followed by `ajQ` is isomorphic to the line bundle of the relative effective Cartier divisor of the point $x$ tensored with the ideal module of the divisor of the cusp section at $t$. The Poincaré bundle of `hDQ` is isomorphic to the base change to $\mathbf Q$ of the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of `hD` (`hpoinc`). The morphism `kQ` compares the geometric generic fibre with the fibre over $\mathbf Q$ (`hkQ₁`, `hkQ₂`), and `ajbar` is the resulting morphism from the curve model `𝔛.Meta.C` to $O.G$ (`hajbar`), lying over the generic point (`hajbar_over`); `εbar` is a $\overline{\mathbf Q}$-point of `𝔛.Meta.C` mapping to the cusp section (`hεbar`) and sent by `ajbar` to the identity section (`hεbar_aj`). Finally $O.\mathrm{pts}$ is a homomorphism for the relative group law supplied by `hD` (`hpts_law`), and `hAJ` states that for any $\overline{\mathbf Q}$-points $x$ and $s$ of `𝔛.Meta.C` with $s$ over the cusp there is a degree-zero divisor $D_v$ equal to the difference of the places of $x$ and of $s$ with $O.\mathrm{pts}$ of its class given by $x$ followed by `ajbar`.
--
--   **The inertia-invariant Henselian base** (`hRA`, `hRdvr`, `hRirr`, `hRfix`, `hRmax`). $R$ is a Henselian local domain with algebraically closed residue field, faithfully mapping into $\overline{\mathbf Q}$, whose image lies in $Pl$; $R$ is a discrete valuation ring in which $p$ is irreducible; an automorphism $\sigma$ of $\overline{\mathbf Q}$ over $\mathbf Q$ lies in the inertia subgroup of $Pl$ precisely when it fixes the image of $R$ pointwise, and every element of $Pl$ fixed by the inertia subgroup lies in the image of $R$.
--
--   **The $p$-divisible group and its dictionary** (`hΔinj`, `hΔlev`, `hΔgal`, `hΔhecke`). $\mathcal G$ is a $p$-divisible group over $R$ of height $h$ (finite free Hopf algebras `𝒢.level v` of rank $p^{vh}$ with surjective transitions whose kernels are the $p^v$-torsion ideals), and $\Delta$ is an injective additive map from the group of $\overline{\mathbf Q}$-points of $\mathcal G$ (the direct limit of the convolution groups of $R$-algebra maps $\mathcal G.\mathrm{level}\,v \to \overline{\mathbf Q}$) into $J_H(M)$. For each $v$, the image of the level-$v$ points under $\Delta$ is exactly `O.finPts (p ^ v)`; $\Delta$ is equivariant for the Galois action in the sense that it intertwines the action of an $R$-automorphism of $\overline{\mathbf Q}$ with that of the underlying $\mathbf Q$-automorphism; and for every generator symbol $g$ there is a family of $R$-coalgebra endomorphisms $\varphi_v$ of the levels, commuting with the transitions, which induces `genOpH M H S g` on $\Delta$-images.
--
--   **The Raynaud quotient** (`hhB`, `hhB2`, `hψt`, `hψker`, `hψsurj`, `hψred`, `hperiod`). $\mathcal B$ is a $p$-divisible group over $R$ of height $h_B$ with $h = O.\mathrm{toricRank} + h_B$ and $h_B = 2h'$, and $\psi_v : \mathcal B.\mathrm{level}\,v \to \mathcal G.\mathrm{level}\,v$ are $R$-coalgebra maps commuting with the transitions. On points, $\psi$ is surjective, a level-$v$ point of $\mathcal G$ has trivial image exactly when its $\Delta$-image lies in `O.toricPts (p ^ v)`, and if the image point reduces to the identity — meaning $v_{Pl}(\text{point}(a) - \varepsilon(a)) < 1$ for all $a$, with $\varepsilon$ the counit — then so does the original point. The period condition `hperiod` says: for each $v$, each $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup of $Pl$, each $p^v$-torsion class $z$ in $\operatorname{Pic}^0(\overline F_H)$ and each level-$v$ point $y$ with $\Delta(y) = \sigma \cdot z - z$, the $\psi$-image of $y$ reduces to the identity.
--
--   **Scheme-level realisation of the layers** (`hρh`, `hιbase`, `hιcl`, `hιp`, `hιpts`, `hιmul`, `hιt`, `hιhecke`, `hιfin`). $\rho_h : R_p \to R$ is a ring map compatible with the embeddings into $\overline{\mathbf Q}$, and $\iota_v : \operatorname{Spec}(\mathcal G.\mathrm{level}\,v) \to O.G$ is a family of morphisms over $\rho_h$ which are closed immersions into the fibre product, are killed by multiplication by $p^v$ in the group law, induce $O.\mathrm{pts}\circ\Delta$ on points, are multiplicative for the group law over any base algebra, are compatible with the transition maps, and are compatible with the Hecke morphisms $O.\mathrm{hecke}$ for a family of coalgebra endomorphisms realising `genOpH` on $\Delta$-images. The clause `hιfin` states that the morphism $j_v$ obtained from $\iota_v$ into the $p^v$-kernel, base-changed along $\rho_h$, is simultaneously an open and a closed immersion and that its image contains every point lying over the closed point of $R$.
--
--   **Atkin–Lehner on places** (`hwgen`). $w_{\mathrm{gen}}$ is a semilinear automorphism of $\overline F_H$, i.e. a pair of ring automorphisms of $\overline F_H$ and of $\overline{\mathbf Q}$ compatible with the structure map, whose action on places agrees with the geometric action of the Atkin–Lehner isomorphism `𝔛.w` on $\overline{\mathbf Q}$-points of `𝔛.Meta.C`.
--
--   **Specialisation pins.** $\rho : R_p \to Pl$ is a ring map compatible with the embedding into $\overline{\mathbf Q}$ and $\Lambda.\sigma_A = \operatorname{Spec}\rho$ (`hσA`). The hypothesis `hsp` (a long list of compatibility clauses, summarised here) states: given $i \in \{0,1\}$, two $\overline{\mathbf Q}$-points $y_1, y_2$ of `𝔛.Meta.C` each lifted to a section $u_j$ of the $\Gamma_M$-level model over $\operatorname{Spec}\rho$ landing in the smooth locus, compatible special-fibre points $u_{\kappa,j}$ and closed points $P_j$ of the special-fibre curve model matching $u_{\kappa,j}$ through the $i$-th component, a degree-zero divisor $D_v$ equal to the difference of the places of $y_1$ and $y_2$, and an admissible gluing datum $x$ (a pair of degree-zero divisors and a family of units, with the prescribed vanishing at the pairs in `O.ssFinset`) whose first component is $(P_1)-(P_2)$ when $i = 0$ and $0$ otherwise, whose second component is $(P_1)-(P_2)$ when $i = 1$ and $0$ otherwise, and whose unit component is trivial — then there is a section $s$ of $O.g$ over $\Lambda.\sigma_A$ with $O.\mathrm{pts}$ of the class of $D_v$ equal to $s$ restricted along the generic point, and with the restriction of $s$ to the residue point corresponding under $O.\mathrm{ptsSp}$ to the glued class of $x$. The hypothesis `hspΛ` is the analogous pin one level down (also with several compatibility clauses, summarised here): for $i \in \{0,1\}$, points $y_1,y_2$ with lifts $u_1,u_2$ and special-fibre closed points $Q_1,Q_2$ matching them through the degeneracy map `𝔛.π` ($i=0$) or `𝔛.πw` ($i=1$), $D_v$ the difference of the places of $y_1,y_2$ and $D_w$ the difference of the places of $Q_1,Q_2$, there is a section $s_0$ of $\Lambda.f$ over $\Lambda.\sigma_A$ with $\Lambda.\mathrm{pts}(O.\deg\mathrm{Pts}\,i\,[D_v])$ given by $s_0$ over the generic point and with $\Lambda.\mathrm{ptsSp}$ of its restriction to the residue point equal to the class of $D_w$. The hypothesis `hdia0` says that transporting a closed point $P$ of the special-fibre curve model through the diamond automorphism `𝔛.dia0 e` again gives a closed point, whose place is the image of the place of $P$ under the diamond action `diamondActionModL` of the $\Gamma_0(M/p)$-lift of $e$.
--
--   **Reduction of function fields and the unit $d$.** $e_K$ is a ring map from $\overline F'$ (the $\Gamma_N$-level $q$-expansion function field over the residue field of $Pl$) to the $q$-expansion function field over $K$ at level $\Gamma_{H'}(M/p)$, given coefficientwise by the residue-to-$K$ map (`heK`), and $pl_K$ is a map on places with $\operatorname{ord}_{pl_K(v)}(e_K g) = \operatorname{ord}_v(g)$ for all $g$ and $v$ (`hplK`). The unit $d \in (\mathbf Z/M)^\times$ reduces to $p$ modulo $M/p$ (`hd`), and its image or the negative of its image lies in $H'$ (`hdH`); $M/p$ is nonzero.
--
--   **Frobenius, diamond and degeneracy block data.** $F$, $F^{-1}$, $F^{*}$ are additive endomorphisms of $\operatorname{Pic}^0(\overline F')$ with $F$ the Frobenius pushforward `qExpFrobeniusPushforwardModL` (`hF`), $F$ and $F^{-1}$ mutually inverse (`hFinv`), and $F^{*} = p\,F^{-1}$ (`hFstar`); $pb \in (\mathbf Z/(M/p))^\times$ reduces to $p$ (`hpb`) and $\delta$ is the diamond action of $pb$ on $\operatorname{Pic}^0(\overline F')$ (`hδ`). The degeneracy data consists of additive maps $\alpha_i : J_{H'}(M/p) \to J_H(M)$ and sections $\deg\mathrm{Pull}_i$ of $O.g$ over $\Lambda.f$ for $i \in \{0,1\}$ with $O.\mathrm{pts}(\alpha_i x) = \Lambda.\mathrm{pts}(x)$ followed by $\deg\mathrm{Pull}_i$ (`hpull`), each $\deg\mathrm{Pull}_i$ being a homomorphism for the group laws (`hpull_mul`), and the special-fibre block law `hpullsp`: for a section $x$ of $\Lambda.f$ over the residue point, the pair of $\operatorname{Pic}^0$-classes attached to $O.\mathrm{ptsSp}^{-1}$ of $x$ followed by $\deg\mathrm{Pull}_i$ is $(\Lambda.\mathrm{ptsSp}^{-1}x,\ F^{*}\Lambda.\mathrm{ptsSp}^{-1}x)$ for $i = 0$ and $(F^{*}\Lambda.\mathrm{ptsSp}^{-1}x,\ \delta\,\Lambda.\mathrm{ptsSp}^{-1}x)$ for $i = 1$. Further, $\overline W$ is the endomorphism of $J_H(M)$ given by the action of $w_{\mathrm{gen}}$ (`hWbar`), and `hUPgen` is the generic relation $U_p x + \overline W x = \alpha_1(O.\deg\mathrm{Pts}\,0\,x)$ for all $x \in J_H(M)$.
--
--   **The root function $\Psi$.** $\iota_K : Pl \to K$ is a ring map whose kernel is the set of elements of valuation $< 1$ (`hιK`) and which factors through the residue field (`hιKres`). $\Psi$ assigns to each $p$-torsion class $x$ of $\operatorname{Pic}^0(\overline F_H)$ an element of the $q$-expansion function field over $K$ at level $\Gamma_{H'}(M/p)$, subject to: `hΨ`, which provides for each $x$ a degree-zero divisor $D$ representing $x$, a nonzero $f \in \overline F_H$ and a Laurent series $y$ over $Pl$ with $p\,(w_{\mathrm{gen}}\cdot D)(v) = \operatorname{ord}_v f$ for every place $v$, with the Laurent expansion of $f$ the coefficientwise image of $y$ under $Pl \hookrightarrow \overline{\mathbf Q}$, with the coefficientwise residue reduction of $y$ nonzero, and with the Laurent expansion of $\Psi x$ the coefficientwise image of $y$ under $\iota_K$; `hΨmul`, that $\Psi(x + x') = c\,g^p\,\Psi(x)\Psi(x')$ for some $c \in K^\times$ and some $g$; `hΨnz`, that $\Psi x \neq 0$; `hΨord`, that $p \mid \operatorname{ord}_v(\Psi x)$ at every place $v$ outside the supersingular set `ssPlacesQExp`; and `hΨfin`, that $x$ lies in `O.finPts p` precisely when $p \mid \operatorname{ord}_v(\Psi x)$ at every supersingular place $v$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** For every $p$-torsion class $x$ of $\operatorname{Pic}^0$ of $\overline F_H$ over $\overline{\mathbf Q}$ such that the corresponding element of $J_H(M)$ lies in the image, under the projection [`TateModule.proj p (JH M H) 1`](def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L122) to the entry of index $1$, of the additive subgroup underlying the corner submodule $e_{i_0}\cdot\mathrm{T}_p J_H(M)$ (the range of multiplication by the idempotent $e_{i_0}$ on the Tate module), and such that this element lies in `O.finPts p`, the following two assertions are equivalent:
--
--   (i) $(\Psi x)^{-1}\, d(\Psi x) = 0$ in the module of Kähler differentials of the $q$-expansion function field over $K$ at level $\Gamma_{H'}(M/p)$ relative to $K$, with $d$ the universal derivation;
--
--   (ii) there exists a level-$1$ point $y$ of $\mathcal G$ over $\overline{\mathbf Q}$ with $\Delta$ of its image in the group of points equal to $x$, and $y$ reduces to the identity, that is, $v_{Pl}\big(\mathrm{toAlgHom}(y)(a) - \varepsilon(a)\big) < 1$ for every $a \in \mathcal G.\mathrm{level}\,1$, where $\varepsilon$ is the counit composed with $R \to \overline{\mathbf Q}$.
--
--   This is the Serre $\operatorname{dlog}$ criterion for the layer of a $p$-divisible group inside the finite part of the Néron model of $J_H(M)$ at a prime $p$ exactly dividing $M$: on the ordinary corner of the $p$-torsion, the logarithmic derivative of the Raynaud root function $\Psi$ vanishes exactly at those classes coming from a level-one point of $\mathcal G$ that reduces to the identity. It feeds the construction of the map from $p$-torsion to supersingular polar differentials used in the level-lowering step at $p$.
